`$md` applies Moth's small Markdown flavour.

It is intentionally not a full CommonMark implementation.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    content = [$md:
        # Title

        Use **clear names** and @./other-page (helpful links).
    ]
]

### Supported block forms

- headings: `#` through `######`
- paragraphs separated by blank lines
- unordered lists: `-` items
- ordered lists: `1.` items
- links: `@./relative-path (link text)`, `@/site-route (link text)` or `@https://... (link text)`
- emphasis: `**bold**` and `*italic*`

### Automatic heading ID rules

A static heading renders an `id` from its complete visible label. Emphasis markers add nothing, inline code contributes its content without backticks and a Moth link contributes its label rather than its target. HTML-sensitive text contributes its visible characters before escaping.

A heading with a dynamic expression or child template anywhere in its label is opaque: it renders no `id` and reports no warning. A label with no letters or numeric characters also renders no `id`.

Normalisation lowercases Unicode text, keeps Unicode letters and numeric characters, folds each run of whitespace, `_` and `-` into one `-` and drops other punctuation. It never leaves a leading or trailing `-`. There is no transliteration and no Unicode normalisation, so NFC and NFD spellings of one text can produce different IDs.

Equal labels produce equal IDs. There are no suffixes, no registry and no diagnostic, so keep linkable labels unique on each page.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    content = [$md:
        See @#getting-started (Getting started).
        See @./guide#getting-started (Getting started).
        See @/docs/guide#getting-started (Getting started).
    ]
]

The first target stays on the same page, the second addresses a relative page and the third addresses a site route. A site-route target keeps its fragment after the origin prefix.

### Link targets

A Moth-aware link target is a URL, never a file value. It is never resolved against the filesystem, copied, watched or tracked. Markdown link parsing is not file discovery. A file value inside `$md` is written as an ordinary nested Moth expression instead, not as a link target. See @../resources/#file-values (File values).

A target beginning with a single `/` is a site route. It renders through the site root, so the project's configured `origin` is applied for you.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    content = [$md:
        Read the @/docs/packages (Packages) reference.
    ]
]

Under `origin #= "/moth"` that renders `href="/moth/docs/packages"`, and `href="/docs/packages"` when no origin is configured. This is the same site-root piece that bare `@/` produces in expression position. See @../resources/#file-paths (File paths).

Every other target renders literally:

- `./` and `../` relative targets
- `#` fragment and `?` query targets
- `//` protocol-relative URLs
- `scheme:` absolute URLs such as `https://`

A relative target is already correct from the page that contains it, and an absolute or protocol-relative URL addresses another origin entirely, so neither takes the site root.

### Link labels

Separate a link target from its parenthesised label with spaces or tabs, or one line break with optional spaces or tabs on either side. At least one separator character is required. LF, CRLF and lone CR each count as one line break. A second line break or intervening non-whitespace content prevents association. Links remain within the current inline block and cannot cross an opaque template or expression boundary.

### Inline code

Inline code uses paired isolated single backticks on one Markdown line.

It renders as a normal HTML `code` element.

Inside an inline code span:

- authored whitespace is preserved
- HTML-sensitive authored characters are escaped
- emphasis is not parsed
- links are not parsed

Not supported:

- empty code spans
- repeated backtick runs
- unmatched backticks
- multiline code spans
- variable-length delimiters
- fenced code blocks
- Markdown-level backtick escaping

Use `$code(language)` for highlighted code blocks.

### Dynamic content boundaries

A dynamic expression anchor may appear inside a parent-authored code span.

`$md` does not inspect that expression's rendered output.

A child template is an opaque formatting boundary.

A parent Markdown span cannot begin before a child and end after it.

### Tables

Markdown pipe-table syntax is not part of Moth Markdown.

Inside `.mtf` content, use a structured list.

When a website page needs a visual table, build it in the owning
`.moth` page with Moth table helpers.

Do not rely on pipe-table text rendering as a table.

### Related content formats

Moth template `.mtf` files use an implicit compile-time `$md` template.

Plain `.md` files use the separate raw Markdown content path.

See @../moth-templates (Moth template files) and @../markdown (Plain Markdown) for those file
contracts.
